WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.052
Google Cloud offers a
first-class experience

00:00:03.052 --> 00:00:04.510
for Windows workloads
that can help

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.520
you reduce your license costs.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.490
Today, we are going to migrate
and modernize a legacy web

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.430
application built on the .NET
Framework to Google Cloud

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.950
Platform without
changing the source code.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.220
Doing so can make your apps
more scalable and more reliable

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.340
while helping to reduce
operations costs.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.970
We're starting with this
familiar e-commerce shop.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.190
It was built using the .NET
Framework version 2.0 and still

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.150
works.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.860
We'll take the existing
application code

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.780
and move it to managed SQL
server and Windows containers

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.140
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.760
The original Pet
Shop architecture

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.060
is a typical application
deployed on a single VM.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.630
It has two tiers, a presentation
layer which is .NET hosted

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.230
on IIS on Windows and a data
layer with four SQL Server

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.260
databases deployed
on the same VM.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.300
We want to modernize
the presentation layer

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.820
to leverage the scalability
and resiliency of Kubernetes.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.420
By using containers, we
can reduce the footprint

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.720
of the application and make it
agile for both day-to-day work

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.370
and future development.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.440
But first, we want to
migrate the data layer

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.600
to a managed SQL Server using
Cloud SQL for SQL Server.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.050
In the Google Cloud
Console, we'll

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.265
create our managed
SQL Server instance.

00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:17.390
There are several details
you will need to provide,

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.080
such as the name of the instance
and the region you prefer.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.530
Be sure to create your database
instance in the same region

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.070
where you will run your
Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.670
Once the instance is created, we
need to migrate the databases.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.120
With Import, we can restore an
existing database backup file.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.100
In this case,
we'll restore a BAK

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.370
file we already uploaded
to a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.940
We'll select our file,
give it a name, and import.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.730
Once we do this for each of
the four original databases,

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.580
we can see the SQL Server
instance and the databases

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.830
right here from
the Cloud Console.

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:52.780
With Cloud SQL
for SQL Server, we

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.460
can use some of
our favorite tools,

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.270
such as Microsoft SQL
Server Management Studio.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.370
We'll need to use SQL
Proxy to help ensure

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.090
the connection between
our local machine

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.270
and Cloud SQL is secure.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.460
Once SQL Proxy is
installed, we'll

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.229
launch it and point to the
instance we just created.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:10.930
As you can see,
the proxy is saying

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.180
it is ready for connections.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.900
We'll use SSMS.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.070
We'll provide
authentication details.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.770
Note that the server
name is localhost--

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.200
this is because we
use SQL Proxy, which

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.220
abstracts the actual address
of where the server is located.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.880
After this connection
is established,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.900
we can see the databases
and interact with them.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.890
We have now migrated
our data layer to GCP.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:38.230
That means we can turn our
attention to the presentation

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.195
layer and the .NET
web application.

00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:43.570
Now, we don't need to change
anything in the existing web

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:44.360
app code.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.780
All we need to do is
create a Docker file.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.750
With Docker, containerization
of our app is just three steps.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.300
First, base off of the
ASP.NET 3.5 base image.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.690
Second, step into the
working directory of IIS.

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.360
And third, copy the
content of this application

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:02.650
into the container.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.810
To build the container
image using the Docker file

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.090
we just made, we'll
run the command docker

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.140
build on a Windows machine
that has Docker CLI installed.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.930
Then, to push this new container
image into the Google Container

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.690
Registry, we'll use docker push.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.370
The Google Container
Registry is what

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.840
will allow us to reference this
image in a Kubernetes cluster.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.140
Before we deploy the web app,
we need to resolve one issue--

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.550
how to allow the web app to
communicate with the databases.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.340
Our new architecture is
built around containers.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:36.700
We can install the Cloud
SQL Proxy as a sidecar

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.540
alongside our web app.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:41.200
This means that the web
app and Cloud SQL Proxy

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.930
will be on the same network,
and this will allow the web app

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:48.170
to communicate with the database
using localhost as a server.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.560
So we'll need to update the
web.config of our web app

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.680
so it uses localhost in the
SQL Server connection strings

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.360
and then rebuild
our container image

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.820
and push it to the
Container Registry.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.880
We'll also build a container
image for the SQL Proxy.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.880
First, we'll need a
SQL Proxy Docker file.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.670
This time, we'll base off
of Microsoft base container

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:09.280
image for Windows
Server 2019 Core,

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.760
then add SQL Proxy to that,
and then invoke the proxy

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:15.930
and point it to the
SQL Server instance.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.070
In Docker, we'll build
a container image

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:19.885
and then push this
image into the registry.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:25.420
We now have two
images, Pet Shop IIS

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:26.980
is the image of
the web application

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.680
that was containerized,
and SQL Proxy Sidecar

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.740
is the SQL Proxy that allows the
app to connect to the managed

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:33.980
SQL Server.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:36.220
We'll deploy the application
and the SQL Proxy

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.290
on the same Pod in a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.650
But first, we need to
provide a YAML file that

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.140
describes the state we
want for the containers.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.890
Fortunately, we can use a fairly
standard boilerplate YAML.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:50.260
The important part
is the node selector,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:52.810
where we can indicate that we
want to deploy into a Windows

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:53.745
node pool.

00:04:53.745 --> 00:04:55.120
This means that
we want to deploy

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.120
our containers as Windows
containers running on a Windows

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.290
node.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.180
Then we list the containers we
want to deploy, the Pet Shop

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.970
IIS and SQL Proxy Sidecar
images we just made.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.760
Finally, we'll expose
this application

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.980
using an external IP.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.350
We'll use kubectl to
deploy our containers

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.420
and check the status
of our web app.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:16.580
Apply starts the deployment.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.810
Get services lists the external
IP address of the web app.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.060
Get deployments lists
our deployment, now

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.730
living in the cloud.

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:28.910
And get pods list the Pods,
showing that the application is

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:31.020
up and running.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.020
At this point, we've
modernized our web app

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.350
into a Kubernetes-managed
app running in Pods with one

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:39.050
container for the .NET app and
a second container for the SQL

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.170
Proxy with secure connections
to the managed SQL Server

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:43.340
database.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.140
The advantage of an
architecture like this

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:49.910
is that we can scale to two Pods
with a single kubectl command.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:51.680
Check the documentation
to get started

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.540
and try it out for yourself.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:56.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

